From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Elsbrock <simon@iodev.org>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:40:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 210/211] ASoC: mediatek: add the MT8519 audio front end,
 ported to 6.12 (compile-only)

The MT8519 audio front end -- the ADR's named hard risk -- from Amazon's 5.4
release, forward-ported to 6.12's ASoC. The DSP path is left out (Pinna does
its audio in userland): nearly all of it is behind CONFIG_MTK_HIFIXDSP_SUPPORT
already, so the direct ETDM PCM paths build without it. Amazon's AFE is built
on their vendor common-AFE layer (which has memif fields upstream lacks), so
that layer is carried locally as vendor-* to avoid colliding with 6.12's
upstream sound/soc/mediatek/common/.

The 6.12 ASoC migration: rtd->cpu_dai -> snd_soc_rtd_to_cpu; the dai
rate/channels/sample_bits reads come from the substream runtime; the component
PCM ops move to .pointer/.ack/.pcm_construct/.pcm_destruct and 6.12's component
.copy; dai_driver suspend/resume and the symmetric_* renames; the PM QoS
cpu-latency API; a monotonic ktime timestamp for the latency control. The
.copy keeps Amazon's clock boost -- it raises the audio bus clock to UNIVPLL_D6
for the span of the copy so the CPU keeps up under load, then restores 26 MHz.

One latency control dropped a call to the private snd_pcm_update_hw_ptr()
(not exported to modules); it reads the core's maintained hw_ptr instead. NOT
RUN ON HARDWARE -- audio is exact register/clock/codec programming that only a
device validates. The cannoli machine driver (mendocino) is a follow-on.
